body:
I suppose the concern is that web developers have written sanitizer code in the past with certain assumptions in mind. 

For example, the following code would remove the `form` element, it would remove any inline event handlers by default, and it would remove the `formmethod` and `formaction` attributes, but it would not remove the `action` and `method` attributes from the button.

```js
const dirtyHtml = `<form action="/new" method="POST">
            <button>New</button>
            </form>
            <button onclick="alert(\'XSS\')" formaction="/new" formmethod="POST" action="/new" method="POST">New</button>`;

const mysanitizer = new Sanitizer({
            elements: ["button"],
            removeAttributes: ["formaction", "formmethod"]
   })
document.getElementById('target').setHTML(dirtyHtml, {sanitizer: mysanitizer});
```

One more thing worth thinking through: the older browser transition story.

### Polyfill safety in older browsers

Supporting browsers would treat `_self` as a reserved value and never do an id lookup. Older browsers won't know that though, so they'll just look for `<form id="_self">` in the DOM as normal. If an attacker can inject a `<form id="_self">` before yours in the document they could add hidden inputs that get submitted along with your button's request. They can't touch the `formaction` since the button's own attribute wins, but they could inject extra parameters or mess with `enctype`/`target`. Though realistically if an attacker can inject arbitrary HTML forms into your page you've already lost, this is pretty far down the list of things they'd bother with.

The straightforward fix is to always place the polyfill as the very first element in `<body>`, claiming the id before any UGC gets a chance. This script handles both head and body placement and detects native support, so it becomes a no-op once browsers adopt the feature:

```js
function ensureSelfForm() {
    const btn = document.createElement('button');
    btn.setAttribute('form', '_self');
    if (!btn.form) {
        if (document.body) {
            const form = document.createElement('form');
            form.id = '_self';
            document.body.insertAdjacentElement('afterbegin', form);
        } else {
            document.write('<form id="_self"></form>');
        }
    }
}
ensureSelfForm();
```

The detached button trick uses the browser's own form association logic to sniff native support. If `btn.form` is non-null, `_self` is already reserved and nothing needs to happen.

### Longer term

`form="_self"` is polyfillable today precisely because older browsers treat it as an id lookup, but that same property means there's a small residual risk in older browsers as long as they exist. As adoption matures and developers stop shipping the polyfill, that older browser tail becomes unprotected.

A cleaner long-term option might be a boolean attribute like `formself`:

```html
<button formself formaction="/logout" formmethod="post">Logout</button>
```

Older browsers just see an unknown attribute and the button renders as a plain inert button. No id lookup, no injection surface, just silently does nothing. The two spellings could coexist as complementary paths:

- `form="_self"` for the transitional period, polyfillable, works in all browsers today
- `formself` for when you want it to fail safe with no polyfill required

Authors could shift from one to the other as their support targets allow. Probably overkill to spec both upfront but worth keeping in mind as a natural follow-on.

Also `_self` is just what came to mind first given the `target=` precedent, but there are other reasonable options: `_own`, `_button`, or `_this` all read naturally as `form="_own"` etc. Happy to defer on naming, just wanted to show the concept isn't tied to that specific value.
